samurai@cs.mcgill.ca (Darcy BROCKBANK) writes:

>As for all the safeguards against typing errors, I disagree with 
>addind them. Nethack only lists character experiece level, but YOUR
>expeience level is the main factor towards winning. A good player does
>not make typing goofs, he is too careful.

Well, it all comes down to philosophy then.  IMHO, nethack is a fantasy
game, and the ideal implementation would minimize real-world
concerns like typeahead, baud rate, and other such boring things.
I certainly don't play a fantasy game to think about computers!

Nethack is full of danger.  But I get a lot more excited trying to
figure out how I'm gonna get past a room full of pissed-off orcs
than I get trying to avoid hitting the "y" key by mistake.

To exaggerate, suppose you had to type "north", "northwest", etc.
to move around, "attack southwest" to fight, and so on.  Would it
take away from the fun?  If so, you see my point.  The more
effectively the interface releases you from the concerns of an
imperfect computer, the more fully you are able to immerse yourself
in the fantasy, which [again, IMHO] is the whole point of the game.

If I want to brush up on typing, I'll play typing tutor!

